    content = [[Prehistory is the longest period of human history, spanning from the appearance of the first hominids to the invention of writing. It is traditionally divided into the Palaeolithic, Neolithic and Metal Ages.

The Palaeolithic extends from about 2.5 million years ago to 10,000 BC. It is the period of hunter-gatherers who made use of all the resources offered by their natural environment. This period is subdivided into:

Lower Palaeolithic: The period in which the first stone tools appear, pebbles worked with a single edge.

Middle Palaeolithic: A development of knapping techniques takes place, such as Levallois and discoid knapping, which allowed the production of more regular flakes for making various tools. Among these, the biface stands out, a stone tool worked on both sides, used for cutting, scraping and digging.

Upper Palaeolithic: At this time, the blade knapping technique developed, making better use of flint and producing more varied and specialised tools, including arrowheads, scrapers, burins, perforators and cutting blades.

Materials and Tools: The material used in the Palaeolithic was stone, mainly quartzite, limestone and flint, which were worked using percussion. One stone was struck against another to obtain fragments or flakes, which could also be used to produce a wide variety of instruments. From the Upper Palaeolithic onwards, bone and antler also began to be used to make harpoons, needles and points or spears, demonstrating greater technical sophistication. All these tools were essential for survival, hunting, food processing and clothing manufacture.

Art and beliefs: During the Upper Palaeolithic, ritual burials accompanied by grave goods became established and developed. Furthermore, artistic expressions, which reached their peak in rock art, became a new form of expression that reveals the high degree of complexity achieved by our species. The engraved and painted plaques from the Cova del Parpalló (Gandia, Valencia) are an excellent example of portable art.]]
