    content = [[Between 10,000 BC and 4,500 BC, the first sedentary communities began to develop in the Near East, with an economy based on agriculture and livestock. This stage represents one of the most momentous changes in human history, fostering the growth of human groups and their social complexity, as well as the emergence of new beliefs. This period is characterised by major technological innovations such as the production of pottery, polished stone tools, and the development of new types of implements for agricultural and livestock activities.

Technology and Materials: If the Palaeolithic was defined by knapped stone, the Neolithic is defined by polished stone. The polishing technique consists of rubbing or grinding the surface of stones (such as diorite, basalt or jade) against other abrasive stones until a smooth surface is achieved. This technique, slower than percussion, allowed stronger and more effective edges for the new agricultural tasks, such as hoes and, especially, polished axes for felling forests and preparing fields.

Another major contribution of this period was the appearance of pottery. Ceramic vessels allowed the storage of surplus grain and liquids, as well as the processing, cooking and consumption of food. Soon, these clay objects, moulded and fired, acquired an artistic as well as a utilitarian character.

Cardial Pottery: One of the most distinctive forms of early Neolithic pottery in the Mediterranean is Cardial (or impressed) ware. Its name comes from the use of the toothed edge of a marine shell, Cardium edule (the cockle), to make decorative impressions on the soft clay before firing. These geometric designs, often in horizontal bands, are a key cultural marker of the expansion of early Neolithic populations along the coasts of southern Europe. Other distinctive decorations of this period included relief decoration, incised decoration made with awls, impressed decoration with bone combs, and ochre painting.]]
