Smelting: The main technical innovation was smelting. The process first required the extraction of ores (copper and cassiterite tin) and their smelting in furnaces at high temperatures. The molten material was then poured into stone moulds to shape different objects: rectangular-section axes, chisels, arrowheads, riveted daggers, metal awls and personal ornaments such as bracelets.

Characteristic ceramic forms of the Valencian Bronze: The 'Valencian Bronze' has its own characteristics that distinguish it from neighbouring cultures such as 'El Argar'. These differences are also visible in the forms of its pottery. Valencian Bronze pottery is handmade and generally undecorated. The most common ceramic forms are jars, cheese moulds, casseroles, pots, bowls and twin vessels with basic functions of storage, processing, cooking and food consumption. In later phases, carinated vessels appeared, with a profile characterised by a sharp change of angle. Other materials such as bone and ivory were used to make the characteristic V-perforated buttons. All these materials bear witness to an economy based on agriculture and livestock, complemented by a developing metallurgy.]]
