    content = [[Between the 6th and 1st centuries BC, the Mediterranean coast of the Iberian Peninsula was home to a group of peoples whom classical authors called 'Iberians'. Although they shared common features such as their language, they never constituted a true political unity and occasionally engaged in conflicts among themselves and with other neighbouring peoples. The ruling classes exercised political and economic control from the cities. Around them, rural settlements (farmsteads and labour houses) were established for the exploitation of agricultural and livestock resources, which were the basis of their economy. Forts and watchtowers were placed at strategic points in the territory for surveillance and defence of borders. The family and its client networks were the foundation of a clearly hierarchical society.

Pottery: The potter's wheel and the two-chamber kiln allowed the production of pottery with very thin walls and homogeneous finishes. The earliest decorative motifs were geometric, and over time vegetal, animal and human elements were added, until two great artistic circles emerged: the narrative style of Liria and the symbolic style of Elche-Archena. Their wide variety of forms reflects a broad range of uses (pantry, transport, cooking, ritual, etc.).

At sites such as Tossal de Sant Miquel (Llíria), vessels with rich figurative decoration have been found, depicting everyday, ritual or warlike scenes, such as processions of warriors, dances, musicians or mythical animals. Their informative and artistic value makes them a fundamental testimony of Iberian culture. At the same time, luxury tableware from the central Mediterranean was imported and sometimes imitated. This pottery, known as black-glazed or Campanian ware, is distinguished by its highly refined paste and dark, almost metallic sheen (achieved by special firing in a two-chamber kiln), imitating the forms of prestigious metal tableware.

The Iberians were the first peninsular peoples to mint coins widely at local mints such as Arse or Kelin, using their own alphabet. The most popular and characteristic design on the reverses of bronze coins (such as the as and its fractions) is the horseman. This figure, typically facing right and shown galloping or with a spear, symbolises the importance of the horse. This animal was a clear symbol of social status, and occasionally some have been found buried alongside their owners' tombs. The most common obverses show a male figure, usually bearded and always facing right.]]
