    title = "PREVENTIVE CONSERVATION AND RESTORATION",
    content = [[The conservation of cultural heritage, especially archaeological objects, is structured around two fundamental and complementary disciplines: preventive conservation and restoration. Understanding the actions of both is essential to guarantee their preservation for future generations.

The main objective of preventive conservation is not to intervene directly on the object, but to act on its environment to slow down its deterioration and prevent future damage. It is a global and forward-looking strategy that seeks to minimise risks before they occur.

In contrast, restoration consists of a direct intervention on the object to stop an active degradation process, consolidate its structure or reintegrate its parts in order to facilitate its reading and interpretation.

Within preventive conservation, environmental control is a cornerstone. Factors such as intense light are extremely harmful, as they can cause irreversible fading, brittleness and accelerated ageing in organic materials such as wood, textiles and pigments.

Equally critical is the control of relative humidity. A high humidity level drastically accelerates the corrosion of metals and promotes the growth of fungi and mould, which can irreversibly damage a wide variety of materials. The goal is not to create an extremely dry environment, but to maintain stability within optimal parameters for each type of material. Preventive conservation measures also extend to storage and packaging solutions.

The use of stable materials, such as acid-free cardboard, is a fundamental practice. Its purpose is to prevent the acidic components present in conventional packaging from transferring to the objects and accelerating chemical degradation reactions such as paper acidification or metal corrosion.

When restoration is necessary, one of the most important ethical principles is reversibility. This means that the materials and techniques applied (adhesives, consolidants, etc.) must be able to be removed in the future without damaging the original object. This principle aims to ensure that current interventions do not compromise the possibilities of future specialists, who will surely have even more advanced techniques and knowledge.

In conclusion, preventive conservation and restoration form an inseparable binomial. The former constitutes the basis of responsible management, creating a stable environment that reduces the need for direct interventions. The latter acts as a specialised tool when deterioration is already present, always applied with scientific rigour and respect for the materiality and history of the object. Together, they ensure that our archaeological heritage endures over time.]]
